<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Blowin' in the Wind (Peter, Paul and Mary)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover; the most successful version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Golden Folk Hits (Bobby Darin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Och vinden ger svar (Tore Lagergren)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Swedish translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die Antwort weiß ganz allein der Wind (Marlene Dietrich)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sam Cooke at the Copa (Sam Cooke)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Change Is Gonna Come (Sam Cooke)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">inspired by the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Blowin' in the Wind (Stevie Wonder)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover; number one on the R&amp;B charts</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">From Every Stage (Joan Baez)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">live cover, used in the film Forrest Gump (1994)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Long and Whining Road (Public Enemy)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2007</span><span class="sg-chain-h">alluded to</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Westboro Baptist Church</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mario + Rabbids Kingdom Battle (video game)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ionic Original disc (Bob Dylan, produced by T Bone Burnett)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new recording, sold at Christie's for $1.78 million</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">No More Auction Block (African American spiritual)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody adapted, by Seeger's and Dylan's accounts</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bound for Glory (Woody Guthrie)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible source of the theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Book of Ezekiel 12:1-2</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Biblical rhetoric, as the critic Michael Gray suggests</span></li></ul></section>
